Round 4 independently held R1-1, R1-2, R1-3, R1-4, R2-1 and approved Q1.
Its final red gate proved the regressions detect weakened launcher and policy
behavior. It also reproduced the new issue below.

R4-1 [P2]: CDPATH breaks valid relative round paths
The shell's exported CDPATH changes how cd behaves. In this launcher, directory
resolution captures both cd and pwd output. A successful cd using CDPATH can
print its destination; the captured value then contains TWO path lines instead
of one. The input/output containment comparison consequently rejects a valid
file instead of launching the reviewer or fixer.

Concrete example from the reviewer's isolated fixture:
  CDPATH=<fixture-repository>
  fm-review-round.sh reviewer --round 1 --in <valid-relative-task-input>
Control with empty CDPATH: success.
Exported fixture CDPATH: exit 1, "output must be next to the round input".
An absolute --in combined with a valid relative --out also fails.
Reproduction (creates fresh fake runtime binaries; no real model call):
  python3 /Users/rymndcs/.codex/review-scratch/round4-k5g1ogzi/cdpath.py
Affected directory captures: bin/fm-review-round.sh:75 and :79.

Likelihood: conditional, requiring a shell that exports CDPATH and use of a
relative path. Generated instructions normally use absolute task paths, so
normal scaffold users are less likely to hit this than manual launcher users.
No fleet frequency was measured. It is a reproduced supported-interface bug.
Who it hurts: a worker or operator invoking the launcher with valid relative
paths in such a shell. The role never starts, delaying review; the explicit
nonzero refusal prevents it being mistaken for a clean review.
Severity: availability failure, not observed file deletion or false success.
Fix cost: approximately 5-10 minutes to neutralize CDPATH in the launcher's
owned path-resolution contexts and add red-first regressions for relative
input/output, both roles/date paths and the nearby sibling captures.
No fix was made because round-boundary authority remains with firstmate.

